<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-requirements" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Degree requirements</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Restated from the Santa Clara University Undergraduate Bulletin
      2026<ndash/>27, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science. The Bulletin
      is authoritative; this chapter exists so that the planner's rules can be
      checked against their source.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-req-major">
    <title>Major in Mathematics</title>
    <p>In addition to the Core Curriculum for the bachelor of science degree:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><c>CSCI 10</c>, or demonstrated equivalent proficiency in programming.</p></li>
      <li><p><c>MATH 11</c>, <c>12</c>, <c>13</c>, <c>14</c>, <c>23</c>, <c>51</c>, <c>52</c>, and <c>53</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p><c>PHYS 31</c> and <c>32</c>. Students planning to teach in secondary schools may substitute <c>PHYS 11</c> and <c>12</c> with approval of the department chair.</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Seven approved 5-unit upper-division courses in mathematics (<c>CSCI 162</c>
          also permitted), which must include
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>at least one course in analysis: <c>MATH 102</c>, <c>105</c>, or <c>153</c>;</p></li>
          <li><p>at least one course in algebra: <c>MATH 103</c> or <c>111</c>;</p></li>
          <li><p>at least one course from geometry (<c>MATH 101</c>, <c>113</c>, <c>174</c>), discrete mathematics (<c>MATH 176</c>, <c>177</c>), or applied mathematics (<c>MATH 122</c>, <c>125</c>, <c>141</c>, <c>144</c>, <c>146</c>, <c>155</c>, <c>166</c>).</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p><c>MATH 100</c> and courses numbered 190 and above do not count toward the seven.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      At least four of the required upper-division courses must be taken at Santa
      Clara. A single upper-division course can satisfy only one major or minor
      requirement in the department. Students should decide on an emphasis by the
      end of junior year; only one emphasis is allowed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Students planning graduate study in pure mathematics should take
      <c>MATH 105</c>, <c>111</c>, <c>112</c>, <c>113</c>, <c>153</c>, and <c>154</c>.
      Students planning graduate study in applied mathematics should complete the
      applied mathematics emphasis and take <c>MATH 105</c>, <c>144</c>, <c>153</c>,
      <c>154</c>, and <c>155</c>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-req-emphases">
    <title>Emphases</title>
    <p>Each emphasis adds the following to the requirements above.</p>
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Applied Mathematics</title>
        <p><c>MATH 102</c>, <c>122</c>, <c>123</c>; two of <c>MATH 125</c>, <c>141</c>, <c>144</c>, <c>146</c>, <c>147</c>, <c>155</c>, <c>166</c>, <c>178</c> (or an approved alternative); one of <c>MATH 166</c>, <c>147</c>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Data Science</title>
        <p><c>MATH 122</c>, <c>123</c>; <c>CSCI 10</c>, <c>60</c>, <c>61</c>, <c>62</c>, <c>183</c>; <c>CSCI 184</c> or <c>CSEN 178</c>; two of <c>CSCI 127</c>, <c>163</c>, <c>MATH 146</c>, <c>147</c>, <c>166</c>, <c>CSEN 166</c>, <c>169</c>, <c>ECON 174</c>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Financial Mathematics</title>
        <p><c>MATH 102</c>, <c>122</c>, <c>123</c>, <c>125</c>; two of <c>MATH 144</c>, <c>146</c>, <c>147</c>, <c>166</c>; <c>BUSN 70</c>; <c>ACTG 11</c>, <c>12</c>; <c>FNCE 121</c>, <c>124</c>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Mathematical Economics</title>
        <p><c>MATH 102</c>, <c>122</c>, <c>123</c>, <c>146</c>, <c>147</c>; <c>ECON 113</c>; two of <c>MATH 125</c>, <c>166</c>, <c>ECON 170</c><ndash/><c>174</c>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Mathematics Education</title>
        <p><c>MATH 101</c>, <c>102</c>, <c>122</c>, <c>123</c> (or <c>8</c>), <c>170</c>, <c>175</c> (or <c>178</c>), <c>195</c>. The Urban Education minor is strongly advised.</p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-req-minor">
    <title>Minor in Mathematics</title>
    <p>
      <c>MATH 11</c>, <c>12</c>, <c>13</c>, <c>14</c>, and either <c>52</c> or <c>53</c>;
      three approved 5-unit upper-division mathematics courses. <c>MATH 100</c>,
      <c>192</c>, and <c>195</c> do not count toward the minor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-req-prereqs">
    <title>Prerequisites used by the planner</title>
    <p>
      From the course descriptions. <q>Pre/co</q> means the course may be taken in
      the same quarter. <q>Alt.</q> marks courses the Bulletin lists as offered in
      alternate years.
    </p>
